<section class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <h1><span class="eyebrow">Federal &amp; state LDAR rule map</span> Where the leak survey meets the rulebook.</h1>
    <p class="lead">The federal and state LDAR rules that reach upstream, midstream, and downstream sites, and where each one stands as of November 19, 2026.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head">
      <h2>Rules by segment.</h2>
      <p class="lead">Federal rules in force or being revised, and the states on this map that write their own. Many other states adopt the federal rules by reference.</p>
    </div>
    <ul class="stat-row">
      <li><p class="stat">3</p><p>federal LDAR rules reach upstream well sites, tank batteries, and centralized production. 7 states add their own.</p></li>
      <li><p class="stat">7</p><p>federal LDAR rules reach midstream gathering, gas plants, transmission, storage, and terminals. 7 states add their own.</p></li>
      <li><p class="stat">4</p><p>federal LDAR rules reach downstream refineries and petrochemical plants. 2 states add their own.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note-ref">Subpart W (40 CFR 98.233(q)) turns leak survey results into reported emissions. EPA proposed in September 2025 to suspend it until reporting year 2034; reporting year 2025 reports are due March 10, 2027.</p>
  </div>
</section>

